---
import Button from './Button.astro';
import Icon from './Icon.astro';

const sections = [
  { id: 'what-image-redaction-removes', label: 'What redaction removes' },
  { id: 'why-screenshots-leak', label: 'Why screenshots leak' },
  { id: 'ai-image-redaction-software', label: 'How the detection works' },
  { id: 'image-redaction-online', label: 'Online, without an upload' },
  { id: 'redaction-styles', label: 'Four styles, one to trust' },
  { id: 'image-redaction-tool', label: 'What the tool should do' },
  { id: 'pre-share-check', label: 'A short pre-share check' },
];

const linkClass =
  'font-heading text-ink underline decoration-hairline underline-offset-4 transition-colors hover:decoration-ink';

const principles = [
  'Local by default: the file is decoded in the tab, with no upload endpoint.',
  'Editable until export: boxes move, resize, undo, and redo; the download is flattened.',
  'Verified output: a re-scan flags text that still reads as sensitive.',
  'Clean files: fresh PNG, JPG, or WebP exports without the original metadata, plus a size panel that measures the real download.',
  'No account, no watermark, no payment.',
];

const checks = [
  'Look at all four corners, the header, the tabs, and any notification.',
  'Use ink for identifiers and payment details; reserve blur and pixelate for context.',
  'Zoom in and confirm that no text peeks out from behind an edge.',
  'Run Verify, then open the exported file at full size.',
  'Ask whether a crop would be safer than a redaction.',
  'Send only the redacted version. Never follow it with the original.',
];
---

<section
  id="image-redaction"
  class="container-page mt-20 scroll-mt-28 md:mt-28"
  aria-labelledby="image-redaction-title"
>
  <div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[0.78fr_1.22fr] lg:gap-16">
    <div class="self-start lg:sticky lg:top-28">
      <p class="flex items-center gap-2 text-[12px] font-heading leading-none text-muted">
        <span class="h-1.5 w-1.5 rounded-full bg-ink" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        Image redaction
      </p>
      <h2 id="image-redaction-title" class="mt-4 text-h2">
        Image redaction, from the pixels up.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-md text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
        What a good tool removes, how on-device detection helps, and where each style stops.
      </p>

      <nav aria-label="In this guide" class="mt-7 hidden lg:block">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-heading text-muted">In this guide</p>
        <ol class="mt-3 space-y-2.5">
          {
            sections.map((section, index) => (
              <li class="flex gap-2.5">
                <span class="mt-0.5 text-[10px] font-heading text-faint">
                  {String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')}
                </span>
                <a
                  href={`#${section.id}`}
                  class="text-[12px] leading-snug text-muted transition-colors hover:text-ink"
                >
                  {section.label}
                </a>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <div class="mt-7 hidden lg:block">
        <Button href="#editor" variant="soft" size="sm" iconRight="arrow-right">
          Redact an image
        </Button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <article class="max-w-2xl">
      <p class="text-[18px] font-light leading-[1.6] text-ink">
        Image redaction is the work of permanently removing or covering sensitive details in a
        picture before it is shared. A good image redaction tool does three things: it finds the
        information that matters, covers it in a way that cannot be peeled back, and leaves no
        hidden data in the exported file. This page explains how image redaction works in this
        editor, what the on-device detection can and cannot see, and the checks worth making
        before an image leaves your hands.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-9">
        <h3
          id="what-image-redaction-removes"
          class="mt-10 scroll-mt-28 text-[22px] leading-tight first:mt-0"
        >
          What image redaction actually removes
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[15.5px] leading-[1.75] text-ink-soft">
          Redaction is not decoration. A black bar, a pixelated block, a blur, and a crop all
          change an image, but only some of them remove the information underneath. The test is
          simple: after export, the covered detail should not be readable, recoverable, or
          inferable from what remains. If a name, email address, card number, ID number, address,
          date of birth, barcode, or account handle can still be read — or reconstructed from the
          pixels — the image is not redacted yet.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[15.5px] leading-[1.75] text-ink-soft">
          That distinction matters because screenshots are full of combinations. A name on its own
          may be public. The same name beside a postcode, an order number, and the last four digits
          of a card is a package that can unlock an account or support a payment. Cover what
          identifies a person, grants access, or proves a transaction, and treat any two of those
          details together as more sensitive than either one alone.
        </p>

        <h3 id="why-screenshots-leak" class="mt-10 scroll-mt-28 text-[22px] leading-tight">
          Why screenshots hide more than you think
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[15.5px] leading-[1.75] text-ink-soft">
          A screenshot records the whole frame, not the part you meant to capture. Browser tabs,
          the URL bar, notification banners, a signed-in account chip, an avatar, a workspace name,
          a timestamp, and the file name in a window title all travel with the image. The detail you
          noticed first is rarely the only one worth covering.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[15.5px] leading-[1.75] text-ink-soft">
          Image files can also carry metadata: EXIF fields, GPS coordinates, camera and device
          details, and the original file name. Screenshots hold less of it than camera photos, but
          assume anything the recipient does not need should not leave with the file. This editor
          exports a newly encoded image from a canvas, so the original metadata stays behind.
        </p>

        <h3
          id="ai-image-redaction-software"
          class="mt-10 scroll-mt-28 text-[22px] leading-tight"
        >
          How AI image redaction software works here
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[15.5px] leading-[1.75] text-ink-soft">
          The detection in this image redaction software is OCR plus pattern checks, not a
          generative model guessing at faces. Tesseract reads the text already visible in the image
          inside a Web Worker, then the editor looks for emails, Luhn-valid card numbers, phone
          numbers, SSNs, Aadhaar numbers, PANs, GSTINs, IBANs, IP addresses, dates, address
          keywords, and short capitalised lines that look like names. Every match arrives as a
          suggestion you can keep or dismiss.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[15.5px] leading-[1.75] text-ink-soft">
          AI image redaction software is useful as a first pass, not a final authority. The scan can
          miss a face, a QR code, a barcode, a logo, or an unusual name, and it cannot know which
          details are sensitive in your context. Use it to stop hunting line by line, then review
          the frame yourself. All text mode covers every detected line when a busy screenshot makes
          individual judgement calls feel like a gamble.
        </p>

        <h3 id="image-redaction-online" class="mt-10 scroll-mt-28 text-[22px] leading-tight">
          Image redaction online, without the upload
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[15.5px] leading-[1.75] text-ink-soft">
          Most image redaction online tools ask you to send the file to a server, wait for it to
          come back, and trust that the copy was deleted. This one is built the other way around.
          The browser decodes the image into a canvas in the current tab, keeps the pixels in
          memory, and draws every redaction locally. There is no upload endpoint, so there is no
          server copy to delete. The{' '}
          <a href="/privacy" class={linkClass}>privacy page</a>{' '}
          documents every request the site makes.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[15.5px] leading-[1.75] text-ink-soft">
          One request worth knowing about is smart detection. On first use, the page downloads
          Tesseract.js and an English language model. Your image is not part of that request.{' '}
          <a href="#editor" class={linkClass}>Manual redaction</a>, undo, copy, and export work
          without it, and once the page has loaded you can keep editing offline.
        </p>

        <h3 id="redaction-styles" class="mt-10 scroll-mt-28 text-[22px] leading-tight">
          Four styles, and the one to trust
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[15.5px] leading-[1.75] text-ink-soft">
          Ink black draws an opaque, flattened shape over the original pixels. There is no gradient
          to reverse and no pattern to analyse, which is why it is the default for IDs, payment
          details, addresses, and anything that grants access to an account. Pixelate replaces an
          area with averaged colour blocks; it can look decisive while preserving the shape of
          large letters. Blur is the softest option and the weakest guarantee, because lightly
          blurred or pixelated text and faces can sometimes be{' '}
          <a href="/guides/blur-vs-pixelate-vs-black-box" class={linkClass}>
            partially reconstructed
          </a>.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[15.5px] leading-[1.75] text-ink-soft">
          The emoji sticker draws an opaque plate before the emoji, so it covers the area instead of
          floating transparently over it. It suits a face in a casual group chat. Cropping is the
          strongest redaction of all when the surrounding context is not needed: a crop cannot be
          unblurred. For anything that identifies a person, moves money, or opens an account, reach
          for ink or a crop. Save the softer effects for context you would not mind losing.
        </p>

        <h3 id="image-redaction-tool" class="mt-10 scroll-mt-28 text-[22px] leading-tight">
          What a good image redaction tool should do
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[15.5px] leading-[1.75] text-ink-soft">
          The feature list matters less than the boundary around it. A tool worth using should work
          on the file locally, keep every box editable until you export, flatten the result into new
          pixels, and verify its own output before you download. It should also be honest about
          where detection stops instead of implying that a scan is a guarantee.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5 text-[15.5px] leading-[1.75] text-ink-soft">
          Those principles are the reason this editor works the way it does:
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2.5">
          {
            principles.map((principle) => (
              <li class="flex gap-2.5 text-[15px] leading-[1.65] text-ink-soft">
                <span class="mt-0.5 grid h-5 w-5 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-full bg-canvas-soft">
                  <Icon name="check" size={11} />
                </span>
                <span>{principle}</span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[15.5px] leading-[1.75] text-ink-soft">
          The useful question is not how many formats a tool lists. It is whether the file, the
          pixels, and the metadata all end up where you expect.
        </p>

        <h3 id="pre-share-check" class="mt-10 scroll-mt-28 text-[22px] leading-tight">
          A short check before you send
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[15.5px] leading-[1.75] text-ink-soft">
          Before the image leaves your hands, make one slow pass over the whole frame. These checks
          catch most of the expensive mistakes:
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-5 space-y-2.5">
          {
            checks.map((check) => (
              <li class="flex gap-2.5 text-[15px] leading-[1.65] text-ink-soft">
                <span class="mt-[0.6em] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-ink" aria-hidden="true" />
                <span>{check}</span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[15.5px] leading-[1.75] text-ink-soft">
          <a href="/guides/redact-a-receipt" class={linkClass}>Receipts</a>,{' '}
          <a href="/guides/redact-an-id-card" class={linkClass}>IDs</a>,{' '}
          <a href="/guides/work-screenshot-checklist" class={linkClass}>work dashboards</a>, and{' '}
          <a href="/guides/how-to-redact-a-screenshot" class={linkClass}>chat screenshots</a>{' '}
          each have their own guide. The short version is always the same: cover what identifies,
          verify what remains, and send the smallest image that still makes your point.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>
